  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The analysis is theoretical and historical, focusing on conceptual design imaginaries rather than empirical urban data or contemporary case studies.",
      "what": "The paper analyzes three designs by Buckminster Fuller—the Geodesic Dome, the Tetrahedronal City for Tokyo Bay, and Skyrise for Harlem—through the respective urban lenses of architecture, infrastructure, and utopia. It critically evaluates how these mid-twentieth-century concepts intersect with contemporary urban crises and the politics of technological solutions.",
      "why": "It introduces Fuller's design history into modern urban studies, offering a framework to use his speculative designs as provocations for imagining alternative urban futures rather than treating them as literal technological blueprints."
    }
